Sales Performance Analysis: Insight and Recommendations Brief
Scope: SQL analysis of 10,000 orders (2023 to 2025) across 5 product categories, 4 sales channels, 4 customer segments, 4 regions, and 4 payment methods; 49 query outputs covering revenue, profitability, discounting, seasonality, and cross-dimensional performance.
---
1. Overview
The dataset covers a mid-sized retail and wholesale operation with total revenue of 34.16m, total profit of 10.74m, and a blended profit margin of 31.4%. Average order value is 3,416 with roughly 8 units per order. Revenue fell 5.2% in 2024 (11.64m to 11.03m) and recovered 4.2% in 2025 (11.49m), which still leaves it short of the 2023 baseline. Profit margin improved slightly each year, from 31.26% to 31.66%, so the business is stable and profitable but not growing.
The most distinctive structural feature of the dataset is uniformity. Profit margin sits within a narrow 30.6% to 32.6% band across every category, product, channel, segment, region, payment method, and discount level, which points to cost-plus pricing across the entire catalogue. As a result, almost all meaningful variance in the business comes from revenue mix and volume, not from margin differences.
2. Key Findings
Category concentration. Electronics generates 40.6% of both revenue (13.88m) and profit (4.34m), followed by Home Appliances (28.6%) and Furniture (19.8%). Clothing (7.9%) and Office Supplies (3.1%) together contribute barely a tenth of revenue, and the entire bottom-10 product list is drawn from these two categories. The top 10 products by revenue and by profit are identical: five Electronics items (led by Laptops at 3.02m) and five Home Appliances.
The volume-value gap in Clothing. Shirts (3,470 units) and Dresses (3,397 units) are among the highest-volume products in the catalogue, outselling Laptops on units, yet each earns under 0.6m in revenue against the Laptop's 3.02m. Demand for Clothing clearly exists; the price point is what limits its contribution.
Discounting is universal and unproductive. Only 25 of 10,000 orders carried no discount, and the average discount is 12.4%. Margin is essentially flat across all discount bands and is actually highest (32.1%) in the 20%+ band, while that same band produces the fewest orders and the lowest revenue. Discounts are therefore neither driving volume nor protecting margin; they function as an across-the-board price reduction that leaks revenue. Bringing the deep-discount bands back toward the 11 to 15% level would recover an estimated several hundred thousand in revenue at no evident volume cost.
Predictable seasonality. Monthly revenue swings by 25 to 35% month on month, but in a repeating annual pattern. August, October, December, May, and January are consistently strong (peak: August 2023 at 1.15m); February, June, September, and November are consistently weak (trough: February 2023 at 0.70m). The dips are structural, not random, which makes them plannable.
Channels and segments are balanced, with useful pairings. Wholesale narrowly leads channels (8.81m) and Corporate trails (8.18m), a spread of only 7%; Corporate is also the only channel declining year on year (2.99m in 2023 down to 2.57m in 2025) while Retail has grown steadily. Consumer leads segments (8.93m) and Government posts the highest profit per order (1,116) and margin (31.75%). The channel-segment matrix shows Online-Consumer (2.43m) and Wholesale-Corporate (2.33m) as the strongest pairings. Regionally, best channel varies: Retail in the East, Wholesale in the North and South, Online in the West; Government is the top segment in the South while Consumer leads everywhere else.
Regions and payment methods are non-factors. North leads regions by only 4% over East, and the four payment methods each hold almost exactly 25% of revenue with near-identical margins. Neither dimension warrants strategic attention.
3. Recommendations
Reform discount policy first; it is the largest available lever. Cap or approval-gate discounts above 15%, starting with the 20%+ band, and pilot the change in one region against a control. With margins insensitive to discount depth and no volume response visible in the data, this is close to free money.
Redirect any retained discount budget into the weak months. Concentrate promotions and campaigns in February, June, September, and November rather than spreading spend evenly; the strong months do not need help.
Lean into the Electronics and Home Appliances mix. With margins uniform, growth is a mix-and-volume question. Prioritise these categories in inventory, marketing, and the Online-Consumer and Wholesale-Corporate pairings that already perform best.
Fix Clothing pricing rather than its demand. High unit volumes at low revenue suggest room to raise price points, bundle, or introduce premium lines; the customer base is demonstrably there.
Rationalise Office Supplies. At 3.1% of revenue with no margin advantage, the range should either be trimmed or repositioned as an attach-on to larger Furniture and Electronics orders.
Investigate the Corporate channel decline. It is the only channel losing revenue three years running; establish whether the cause is client attrition, pricing, or service before it compounds.
Simplify reporting. Drop regions and payment methods from routine dashboard depth and give that space to discount bands, monthly trend, and category mix, where the variance actually lives.
4. Analytical Caveats
Uniform margins at this precision are unusual in real trading data and most likely reflect cost-plus pricing (or a synthetic dataset); conclusions about margin optimisation are therefore limited, and the analysis correctly shifts its weight onto revenue mix, discount behaviour, and seasonality. Contribution-percentage fields returned NULL in several outputs (a divide or windowing issue in the SQL) and were recomputed manually for this brief.
